from common import *
it('KERNEL-0199','rule','3.4','An arena is one planar subdivision: its faces never overlap and two of its chains that cross share a vertex (§3.6 insertion; §3.10 no two edges cross), so inside an arena every crossing is a junction.',['§3.4 underground layer several arenas','§3.10'],'kernel',['@insertChain','@invariants'],['@undergroundParts'],['arena','every crossing a junction'])
it('KERNEL-0200','rule','3.4','The underground layer several arenas (item 65; J-A65.31): underground systems passing over or under one another without meeting (catacomb gallery beneath a sewer main, undercity chamber below tunnels, quarry workings beneath catacomb galleries) never share an arena: each is its own part of the underground layer keyed { layer: underground, part } (§2.2 PlatKey); the sewer network is part 0 (II.7.2); a further system part number is allocated once, when the system is first created, never renumbered or reused (part of its entities keys and addresses, §2.1, §6.3); which system takes which number is II.7.6; inside one part depth stays per entity (J-A12.45), because what shares a part may meet.',['§3.4 underground layer several arenas'],'kernel (part allocation owned by II.7.6 / V04 underground)',['§2.2 PlatKey','II.7.2','II.7.6'],['V04 underground','II.7','§6.3 addresses'],['undergroundParts','underground part','part 0','J-A65.31'])
it('KERNEL-0201','rule','3.4','Underground parts add no kernel operation: J-A12.32 holds across parts as between ground and underground; a transaction writes the one part its key names; a stage deriving one system reads the ground and every other part only through the services by key (a catacomb under a churchyard reads the ground faces; where a link to another system can land is a pointLocate in that system part); a place where two systems meet, or a stair or shaft joining them, is a record naming one vertex in each part by its role (II.7.6 link, as AccessPoint names a ground point and a kerb vertex, II.7.4), never a vertex two arenas share.',['§3.4 underground layer several arenas'],'kernel',['@crossArenaRead','@pointLocate'],['II.7.4 AccessPoint','II.7.6 links','V04 underground'],['no new op for parts'])
it('KERNEL-0202','rule','3.4','Every entity faces, chains and vertices lie in the one part its Identity.key names (§2.8), so a FaceRef, ChainRef or VertexRef resolves in one arena - with one exception: a link between two parts (II.7.6 UndergroundLink, §2.7), whose two link_end vertices lie one in each part it joins, each end naming its part ({ part; at }) so resolve reads it with that part key, each placed or stripped by a transaction on that part alone; a grouping spanning systems (an undercity neighbourhood and its outskirts, the plots along the sewer tunnels leading into it, II.7.5) is a link between records in different parts, never one entity with faces or chains in two arenas.',['§3.4 underground layer several arenas'],'kernel (record UndergroundLink owned by §2.7 / II.7.6 / V04)',['§2.8 Identity.key','§2.7 UndergroundLink'],['@resolve','II.7.5','V04'],['UndergroundLink','link_end'])
it('KERNEL-0203','rule','3.4','Each underground part has its own index partitions (§3.3), snapshot block (§3.9) and level digests under §6.2 per-arena plats node; a system derivation opens transactions on its own part alone, so re-deriving one system leaves the ground and every other part byte-identical (§3.10 arena-isolation row) except the link_end vertices of the links ending on it: the connection stage (II.7.6) re-derives those links, placing, keeping or stripping each far end by one transaction on each part it touches - a link whose element has no successor is removed, its far link_end row stripped by removeEdges on its VertexRef, which by §2.2 rule 3 dissolves the vertex and restores the edge it split unless other geometry met the vertex since.',['§3.4 underground layer several arenas'],'kernel',['@staticPartition','@snapshotBlock','§6.2'],['@invariants arena-isolation row','II.7.6 connection stage'],['arena isolation','per-part partitions'])
it('KERNEL-0204','mechanism','3.4','Transient arenas (J-A28.31): the composition rungs - a stage-3 compose() and the plan storeys, one at a time (§2.6) - partition rings that are no plat faces (rooms cut into the L4 arena would enter its index partitions and the model digest on every click, so a click would change the model, §4.1, §6.2); they cut on a TRANSIENT ARENA: a one-level scratch subdivision on the world lattice under §3.2 numbers, §3.6 insertion rules and §3.10 invariants, opened for one derivation call and gone at its end.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel (callers owned by §2.6 / V02 buildings, V03 interiors)',['§2.6 composition contract'],['§2.6 compose(), plan storeys','V02 buildings','V03 interiors','§12.7 stage-3 rows'],['transientArena','transient arena','J-A28.31'])
it('KERNEL-0205','type','3.4','type TransientKey = { transient: true; owner: Id (the entity whose derivation opened it); n: number (per call, never persisted) }.',['§3.4 Transient arenas code'],'kernel',[],['@ArenaKey','@transientArena ops'],['TransientKey'])
it('KERNEL-0206','type','3.4','type ArenaKey = PlatKey | TransientKey - what KernelTx.key and CommitRecord.key name, what resolve, pointLocate and faceNeighbours accept; resolveVertexAt, signature and sampleProfile read a plat role rows and take a PlatKey.',['§3.4 Transient arenas code','§3.4 Arenas, keys and services','§3.3 KernelTx.key'],'kernel',['§2.2 PlatKey'],['@KernelTx','@CommitRecord','@resolve'],['ArenaKey'])
it('KERNEL-0207','interface','3.4','transientArena(owner: Id, seed: Ring[] (world lattice rings, counter-clockwise, tiling: interiors pairwise disjoint, two rings meeting only along sides or at vertices; tiled wings share each joint vertices by §2.6 wing-carrying rule 1, while a vertex one ring has on another side splits that side), fixed: { ring; owner }[] (counter-clockwise, faces made before any cut and never cut, each inside one seed ring - a room never spans a joint between wings - interiors pairwise disjoint, a ring coinciding with its seed ring allowed: a stair core bay, a hearth chimney stack, a spanned passage room (item 30), a composer room (a hall, a tower round room: §2.6 Composition.rooms) less any stair bay or chimney stack in it (§2.6 contract of stage 3) and, on a re-cut, the rooms §2.6 interior ladder keeps (item 28 (f))), workLimit: number): KernelResult<TransientKey> - E_DEGENERATE (detail seed | fixed | overlap) names the offending vertex (for overlap, a vertex of the offending ring); the construction moves nothing.',['§3.4 Transient arenas code'],'kernel',['§2.6 wing-carrying rule 1','§2.6 Composition.rooms','@tileRings'],['§2.6 compose(), plan','V03 interiors','V02 buildings','@E_DEGENERATE'],['transientArena()','seed','fixed'])
it('KERNEL-0208','interface','3.4','readBack(key: TransientKey): { faces: { ring; holes: Ring[]; owner: Id | null; fixed: boolean }[]; edges: { a; b: LatticePoint; left: number; right: number | outside (face indices in this result); seed?: { ring; side } (the seed ring whose side the edge lies on with that ring interior on its left - for a joint, whichever of its two rings lies on that side - and which side by that side first vertex index); joint: boolean (on a side two seed rings share) }[] }.',['§3.4 Transient arenas code'],'kernel',['@transientArena'],['§2.6 plan storeys, rooms, walls, openings','§2.6 unit-door rule (§5.3 check)','V03 interiors'],['readBack'])
it('KERNEL-0209','interface','3.4','release(key: TransientKey): void - ends the transient arena; no slot of it outlives release.',['§3.4 Transient arenas code'],'kernel',['@transientArena'],['§2.6 composition callers'],['release'])
it('KERNEL-0210','rule','3.4','Transient arena opening: its L0 faces are the seed rings then the fixed rings, each a face of its own, inserted as exact topology the way §3.9 load inserts stored chunks: a lattice point two rings share is one vertex, a side two rings share is one edge (the wall at the joint between two wings - an interior wall, never a party wall, §2.6), a vertex within the hot pixel (0.71 cm) of another ring side splits that side there, and no attraction moves any of them; an input ring breaking minEdge, clearance or simplicity is refused with E_DEGENERATE (detail seed or fixed) naming the vertex, which F-PLAN tier 1 takes (§5.2), so a planner places an anchor on a wall or at least minEdge from it.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@exactInsertion (§3.9 load)','@minEdge'],['§5.2 F-PLAN tier 1'],['exact topology insertion','joint'])
it('KERNEL-0211','rule','3.4','Transient arena overlap refusal: seed rings that overlap, and a fixed ring that crosses a seed ring side, lies outside the seeds union or overlaps another fixed ring, are refused with E_DEGENERATE (detail overlap): two crossing input sides are refused where the construction meets them, and it then labels every face with how many seed rings and how many fixed rings contain it by the booleans winding walk (§3.7), so a face inside two seed rings, a face inside a fixed ring and no seed ring or inside two fixed rings, and a fixed ring whose inside is more than one face are found exactly, in one walk.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@windingWalk'],['@E_DEGENERATE','§5.2 F-PLAN'],['overlap refusal','winding labels'])
it('KERNEL-0212','mechanism','3.4','The seed clip (tileRings): a caller whose wings overlap (§2.6 rule 4 (iii)) passes them clipped to tile: the faces of one overlay of the storey rings on the arrangement (§3.7 boolean machinery), each face going to the first ring in the order passed (§2.6 clip order) that contains it and each ring faces united; each ring is thereby minus every ring before it (a covered ring has no piece; one cut in two has two), and every ring carries each crossing vertex the overlay placed on its sides, so joints are shared sides vertex for vertex; the overlay moves no vertex where rings keep §2.6 composition contract (at least attractCm apart except at joints). A piece left with a hole (earlier rings inside a later one without reaching its sides) is refused (E_DEGENERATE, detail overlap, naming a vertex of the hole), because a seed is one ring. §2.6 clip order puts a contained wing after its container, and a composed tower wholly inside a wing before that container only where an earlier overlapping wing carries its cut to the container sides, so the refusal marks a caller bug (F-PLAN, §5.2).',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@oneEngine','§2.6 clip order','§2.6 rule 4 (iii)'],['@transientArena','§5.2 F-PLAN'],['seed clip','clip order'])
it('KERNEL-0213','rule','3.4','The opening exact insertion is the one way edges enter a transient arena other than through its face partitioners, as §3.9 load is for a stored plat (J-A28.34).',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@exactInsertion'],['@doors'],['transient door'])
it('KERNEL-0214','rule','3.4','Fixed edges: the seed and fixed rings edges are the arena FIXED EDGES: mergeFaces refuses to delete one (E_STRUCTURAL) and absorbSliver never deletes one (a sliver bounded only by fixed edges stays a face, W_SLIVER_RETYPED, §3.6); no writer moves a vertex there - a wall moves by mergeFaces then splitFace at its new line (the plan moveWall, §7.1), so warp is not offered - which makes §3.10 seed-tiling row hold by construction.',['§3.4 Transient arenas','§3.1 E_STRUCTURAL'],'kernel',['@transientArena'],['@E_STRUCTURAL','W_SLIVER_RETYPED','§7.1 moveWall','@invariants seed-tiling row'],['fixed edge'])
it('KERNEL-0215','rule','3.4','Transient arena op set: its batches are §3.3 transactions with the transient fields (either op form, the caller workLimit); its writers are the face partitioners splitFace, connectHole, radialSplit, ringBand, mergeFaces, absorbSliver, setOwner; booleans, offsetFace, insetRing, obb, lir, sideRuns, skeleton, faceNeighbours and area2 are services on its faces; insertChain, splitThrough, removeEdges, clearFiner, retypeEdges, claimSubFace, corridorUnion, bandUnion, warp and pinVertices are NOT offered on it.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@KernelTx'],['§2.6 callers'],['face partitioners','transient op set'])
it('KERNEL-0216','rule','3.4','A transient arena has no static partitions (its dynamic grid serves every query), no stage ranks, no kept content, no role rows beyond the owners the caller sets, no PlatKey and no entity; it is never persisted (§3.9), digested (§6.2) or frozen (§3.3); no slot of it outlives release. readBack returns rings and edges as values in §3.3 canonical order, so the caller records (plan storeys, rooms, walls, openings, §2.6) depend on the input rings and op sequence alone, whichever worker cut them.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@queries','@canonicalOrder'],['§2.6','§6.2 (excluded)','§3.9 (excluded)'],['transient never persisted'])
it('KERNEL-0217','rule','3.4','A caller may plan its cuts on a coarser module of its own (the plan rung wing-local module, a table row, §2.6): the module is the planner grid, never a second lattice - every point enters through snap and the placement rule, so a cut ends lie on the fixed edges within the hot pixel of their input sides and the tiling holds exactly.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel',['@snap','@placement'],['§2.6 plan module','V03 interiors'],['planner module','never a second lattice'])
it('KERNEL-0218','budget','3.4','Transient arena opening costs O(seed vertices) and each op what the list states, on tens of vertices per storey; §12.7 stage-3 rows count it.',['§3.4 Transient arenas'],'kernel (budget §12.7)',[],['§12.7 stage-3 rows'],['transient cost'])
it('KERNEL-0219','rule','3.4','The input sides are the arena routes (J-A28.34): a side of a seed or fixed ring as passed is an INPUT SIDE; no split changes it; it is the route of every fixed edge on it (as a plat coarser route is the route of Steiner vertices a finer batch places, §2.2 rule 3); the arena grid holds it as one route-edge item, its box the side padded by the hot pixel (§3.3). That route is read by placement and by its grid item and is never the unit of an answer: a query and readBack return each arena edge as its own item (a grid hit on an input side expanding into its pieces meeting the box), keyed by §3.3 canonical key on that edge own endpoints (every vertex of the arena being of its one level), so no two edges share a key and an edge on more than one input side needs no choice.',['§3.4 The input sides are the arena routes'],'kernel',['@queries','@routeEdge'],['@placement','@readBack'],['input side','J-A28.34'])
it('KERNEL-0220','rule','3.4','Every vertex a face partitioner places on a fixed edge (a cut end, a ray, a band cross edge) is placed by §3.6 placement rule against the input side that edge lies on, never a sub-edge an earlier split left: test (i) reads that side line, and on an edge lying on several input sides it reads each (a joint lies on two; a fixed ring side along a seed side on two; a fixed ring side along a joint - a kept room, or the passage room, a span ring both seed and fixed, §2.6 - on three; two fixed rings sides back to back across a joint on four). So every vertex on a fixed edge stands within the hot pixel of the line of each input side it lies on, and a kept ring vertices meet the split test when it re-enters.',['§3.4 The input sides are the arena routes'],'kernel',['@placement'],['@reCut'],['multi-side placement'])
it('KERNEL-0221','mechanism','3.4','A re-cut is the same call (item 28 (f); J-A28.33): the plan derived again for new occupants under §2.6 interior ladder opens a fresh arena from the same seed rings (the shell item 31 geometry floor fixed, §4.11), passes as fixed the rings §2.6 re-cut rule fixes (stair core bay, chimney stacks, passage room, composer rooms, each less a bay or stack it holds, and the rooms the ladder keeps) and cuts the rest; after item 18 mutation the seeds are the mutated shell rings, and a room is kept only where its seed ring (its wing extent at that storey, clipped to tile) is unchanged (§2.6 kept-room rule).',['§3.4 A re-cut is the same call'],'kernel (ladder owned by §2.6 / V03 interiors)',['§2.6 interior ladder','§2.6 re-cut rule','item 31 geometry floor (§4.11)','item 18 mutation'],['V03 interiors','§7.3 least-disruption'],['reCut','re-cut','J-A28.33'])
it('KERNEL-0222','rule','3.4','Kept ring re-entry: a kept ring re-enters exactly on the lattice points it left with and keeps its polygon (every vertex unmoved and in order; any vertex a new wall adds lies within the hot pixel of one of its sides). Its vertices on a seed ring side or another fixed ring side lie within the hot pixel of that side line, so the construction splits the side at each. The only geometry beside it that can differ is such a side straightened (by at most the hot pixel) where an unkept wall ended on it, so the exact insertion accepts it unless that straightening brings the side within minEdge of one of its vertices, which is refused as above (J-A28.34).',['§3.4 A re-cut is the same call'],'kernel',['@exactInsertion','@placement'],['@E_DEGENERATE','§5.2 F-PLAN'],['kept ring re-entry'])
it('KERNEL-0223','rule','3.4','Nothing of an earlier cut is edited, because no arena outlives its call: the model derives the plan as a chain of least-disruption steps (§2.6 canonical chain, J-A28.26; §7.3), each step its own open, cut and read-back, counted by §12.7 plan row. The seed rings never change with the occupants, so the interior changes while the shell cannot; §3.10 seed-tiling row holds on every re-cut as on the first cut. The unit-door rule of §2.6 (its §5.3 check) reads readBack edges: a door edge has the unit room on one side and a circulation face or outside on the other.',['§3.4 A re-cut is the same call'],'kernel',['@readBack'],['§2.6 canonical chain','§12.7 plan row','§5.3 unit-door check','@invariants seed-tiling'],['least-disruption plan chain','unit-door rule'])
add(I)
